1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:04,922
Sam had two more images open side by side: a clean shot of the sock alone, and a

2
00:00:04,922 --> 00:00:07,265
scene of someone mid-shift adjusting one.

3
00:00:07,265 --> 00:00:11,838
Both had come out of the same batch, both looked fine at a glance, and his first

4
00:00:11,838 --> 00:00:14,410
instinct was to run identical checks on both.

5
00:00:14,410 --> 00:00:16,524
That instinct turned out to be wrong.

6
00:00:16,524 --> 00:00:19,610
Nothing about the two images announced the difference.

7
00:00:19,610 --> 00:00:23,954
They were the same resolution, the same brief, generated the same afternoon.

8
00:00:23,954 --> 00:00:28,755
The difference only showed up once he asked what each one was actually for, which is

9
00:00:28,755 --> 00:00:33,441
exactly the question this lecture wants you asking before you generate either kind

10
00:00:33,441 --> 00:00:33,784
again.

11
00:00:33,784 --> 00:00:38,357
One more generation technique before the workshop, and it's really a distinction

12
00:00:38,357 --> 00:00:43,157
worth having clearly in your head: a packshot, the product alone doing the work of a

13
00:00:43,157 --> 00:00:47,844
specification sheet, and a lifestyle scene, the product in a moment doing the work

14
00:00:47,844 --> 00:00:51,273
of a story, are not the same job wearing different lighting.

15
00:00:51,273 --> 00:00:55,903
Confuse which one you're generating, and you'll brief the wrong thing every time.

16
00:00:55,903 --> 00:01:00,475
Which one is which usually isn't hard to tell once you ask what job the image is

17
00:01:00,475 --> 00:01:00,818
doing.

18
00:01:00,818 --> 00:01:04,361
If it's answering "what exactly am I buying", it's a packshot.

19
00:01:04,361 --> 00:01:08,762
If it's answering "what would this feel like to own", it's a lifestyle scene.

20
00:01:08,762 --> 00:01:11,677
Different question, different standard of accuracy.

21
00:01:12,227 --> 00:01:16,362
Here's why the distinction matters more than it sounds like it should.

22
00:01:16,362 --> 00:01:21,147
A packshot often does double duty: it's the ad creative, and it's close enough to

23
00:01:21,147 --> 00:01:26,050
what shows on the product page that a mismatch reads as false advertising, not just

24
00:01:26,050 --> 00:01:26,700
an off day.

25
00:01:26,700 --> 00:01:30,363
A lifestyle scene carries a completely different kind of risk.

26
00:01:30,363 --> 00:01:34,911
Nobody assumes the person in a stock-style lifestyle shot is a specific, real

27
00:01:34,911 --> 00:01:37,274
customer having a specific, real moment.

28
00:01:37,274 --> 00:01:38,456
It has to feel true.

29
00:01:38,456 --> 00:01:42,060
It doesn't have to be literally true the way a packshot does.

30
00:01:42,060 --> 00:01:46,431
Would a customer feel misled if they knew exactly how this image was made?

31
00:01:46,431 --> 00:01:51,334
For a lifestyle scene, almost never, because nobody assumed it was a documentary in

32
00:01:51,334 --> 00:01:52,280
the first place.

33
00:01:52,280 --> 00:01:57,242
For a packshot standing in as the product photo, the honest answer is often yes, and

34
00:01:57,242 --> 00:02:02,027
that's the test worth running before you decide where a given image is allowed to

35
00:02:02,027 --> 00:02:02,618
come from.

36
00:02:02,618 --> 00:02:07,107
Run that same question down your own angle map before you generate anything.

37
00:02:07,107 --> 00:02:12,070
Most rows sort themselves in a few seconds once you ask what job the image is doing,

38
00:02:12,070 --> 00:02:16,973
and the ones that don't sort easily are usually the ones worth a second look before

39
00:02:16,973 --> 00:02:17,623
you decide.

40
00:02:18,173 --> 00:02:21,877
Here's the honest split, applied directly to Harlan's account.

41
00:02:21,877 --> 00:02:26,776
An ad packshot, generated from the same locked reference from Section 3.3, is safe

42
00:02:26,776 --> 00:02:31,615
territory, because it's testing creative, not standing in for the actual purchase

43
00:02:31,615 --> 00:02:32,153
decision.

44
00:02:32,153 --> 00:02:37,112
The product listing photo itself is where this course draws a hard line: that image

45
00:02:37,112 --> 00:02:41,831
is effectively a promise about what arrives in the box, and it should be a real

46
00:02:41,831 --> 00:02:44,102
photo of the real product, every time.

47
00:02:44,102 --> 00:02:48,881
Lifestyle scenes, with or without a face, are safe to generate, carrying forward

48
00:02:48,881 --> 00:02:51,749
exactly the checks Section 3.3 already gave you.

49
00:02:51,749 --> 00:02:55,035
Notice this table doesn't introduce a single new check.

50
00:02:55,035 --> 00:03:00,053
Every "yes" in it still owes you the colour lock, the product-accuracy read, and the

51
00:03:00,053 --> 00:03:02,801
honest face-or-no-face call from last lecture.

52
00:03:02,801 --> 00:03:07,521
What changes here is only the question of which image type this course lets you

53
00:03:07,521 --> 00:03:11,225
generate at all, not how carefully you check the ones it does.

54
00:03:11,225 --> 00:03:16,005
Notice also there's exactly one "no" on the whole table, and it's not a grudging

55
00:03:16,005 --> 00:03:16,602
exception.

56
00:03:16,602 --> 00:03:21,501
The listing photo is the one image in this entire workflow where the cost of being

57
00:03:21,501 --> 00:03:26,281
wrong isn't a wasted test, it's a customer holding something in their hands that

58
00:03:26,281 --> 00:03:28,013
doesn't match what sold them.

59
00:03:28,013 --> 00:03:32,793
Everything else on this table earns its "yes" precisely because getting it wrong

60
00:03:32,793 --> 00:03:33,809
costs a lot less.

61
00:03:34,359 --> 00:03:39,358
Here's what a working prompt actually looks like, carrying forward every discipline

62
00:03:39,358 --> 00:03:42,611
from the last two lectures rather than starting fresh.

63
00:03:42,611 --> 00:03:46,888
The hex lock and reference image from Section 3.3 come along unchanged.

64
00:03:46,888 --> 00:03:51,767
No overlay text, because anything worth saying exactly goes through Section 3.4's

65
00:03:51,767 --> 00:03:52,791
workflow instead.

66
00:03:52,791 --> 00:03:57,731
And notice the instruction against a face close-up: this scene doesn't need one to

67
00:03:57,731 --> 00:04:02,128
do its job, so the safest version of it doesn't generate one to find out.

68
00:04:02,128 --> 00:04:04,899
Read this prompt as a template, not a one-off.

69
00:04:04,899 --> 00:04:09,838
Every lifestyle angle on Harlan's map can start from this same skeleton, with only

70
00:04:09,838 --> 00:04:14,898
the moment and the setting changed: a different break room, a different point in the

71
00:04:14,898 --> 00:04:19,356
shift, the same locked colour and the same discipline about faces and text

72
00:04:19,356 --> 00:04:20,862
underneath every version.

73
00:04:20,862 --> 00:04:25,801
That's the actual efficiency win here, and it's easy to miss under all the detail.

74
00:04:25,801 --> 00:04:30,680
Writing one disciplined template once is what lets twenty lifestyle variants take

75
00:04:30,680 --> 00:04:35,680
minutes instead of twenty separate negotiations with a generator over what "natural

76
00:04:35,680 --> 00:04:38,752
lighting" or "not too posed" should mean each time.

77
00:04:39,302 --> 00:04:44,251
One line worth holding onto after this lecture, because it's the one rule in this

78
00:04:44,251 --> 00:04:45,901
section with no exceptions.

79
00:04:45,901 --> 00:04:50,117
Everything else here is genuinely safe to generate, checked properly.

80
00:04:50,117 --> 00:04:54,638
The product listing photo is not, and that's not caution for its own sake.

81
00:04:54,638 --> 00:04:59,771
It's the one image where "close enough" becomes a promise you can't keep if the real

82
00:04:59,771 --> 00:05:03,681
product doesn't match what a customer saw before they bought it.

83
00:05:03,681 --> 00:05:08,630
If you're ever unsure which side of the line a specific image sits on, default to

84
00:05:08,630 --> 00:05:09,852
the stricter answer.

85
00:05:09,852 --> 00:05:14,740
Treating a borderline case as ad creative when it's really acting as the listing

86
00:05:14,740 --> 00:05:19,872
photo is a mistake that surfaces as a return, a complaint, or worse, only after real

87
00:05:19,872 --> 00:05:21,828
money has already changed hands.

88
00:05:21,828 --> 00:05:26,655
The test is always the same one from earlier in this lecture: does a customer's

89
00:05:26,655 --> 00:05:30,809
actual purchase decision rest on this specific image being accurate.

90
00:05:30,809 --> 00:05:35,820
If yes, it's a listing photo wearing an ad's clothing, and it gets a camera, not a

91
00:05:35,820 --> 00:05:40,830
generator, no matter how good the generated version looks sitting next to the real

92
00:05:40,830 --> 00:05:41,135
ones.

93
00:05:41,685 --> 00:05:46,883
One lecture left before the section recap, and it's the one where you actually run

94
00:05:46,883 --> 00:05:52,145
this on a product of your own, start to finish: brief, batch, colour fix, text fix,

95
00:05:52,145 --> 00:05:55,695
and the packshot-versus-lifestyle call, all in one pass.

96
00:05:55,695 --> 00:06:00,386
Before you get there, do the sort: two columns, twenty rows, five minutes.

97
00:06:00,386 --> 00:06:05,711
Packshot or lifestyle, and one honest check on each: is any row secretly the product

98
00:06:05,711 --> 00:06:08,183
listing photo wearing an ad's clothing.

99
00:06:08,183 --> 00:06:13,508
Pull those out now, while it's a five-minute decision, not after a batch has already

100
00:06:13,508 --> 00:06:14,142
generated.

101
00:06:14,142 --> 00:06:17,882
That sort is the last piece Section 3.6 assembles into B03.
